  {
   "id": "q127",
   "system": "full",
   "answer": "[1] Elevated plasma BCAA levels in people with insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes, as reported in several studies, including [2] and [5], which show that plasma BCAA levels are considerably elevated in plasma and tissues in individuals with obesity and T2D, and that elevated BCAA levels strongly associate with insulin resistance in people with obesity and T2D.\n\n[2] Plasma BCAA levels are elevated in patients with T2D compared to age- and BMI-matched controls without having T2D, and that elevated plasma BCAA levels strongly associate with insulin resistance in obesity and T2D [2]. In humans with insulin resistance, the effect of insulin on reducing muscle protein breakdown is blunted, causing increased muscle wasting, and BCAA levels are reported to be elevated, with the most marked decline seen for BCAA [5]. Plasma BCAAs act as signaling molecules and contribute to the development of insulin resistance in humans, and individuals with impaired or incomplete BCAA metabolism are susceptible to develop insulin resistance. Note: The exact relationship between plasma BCAA levels and insulin resistance in people with type 2 diabetes is still not fully understood, and further research is needed to determine the underlying mechanisms.",
